<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<workout_file>
    <author>Domestique Library</author>
    <name>Rønnestad 30/15 VO2max — 2x13 @ 110% (40 min)</name>
    <description>2 series of 13 x (30s @ 110% FTP / 15s easy), 3 min easy between series. The 15 s recoveries are deliberately too short for oxygen uptake to fall back, so it ratchets up over the first few reps and stays near maximum for the rest of the series — which is the point. Time spent near VO2max is what drives the adaptation, and short on/off reps buy more of it than long intervals at the same average power. The percentage is a STARTING POINT, not the protocol: the source studies prescribed no percentage at all — ride the highest power you can repeat in the LAST series and adjust between series. RPE 16-18. A session you fade through trains fading. Warmup: 10min from 45% to 78% FTP | Microintervals: 2 x 13 x (30s @ 110% / 15s @ 55%) | Cooldown: 8min from 55% to 45% FTP</description>
    <sportType>bike</sportType>
    <workout>
        <Warmup Duration="600" PowerLow="0.45" PowerHigh="0.78" pace="0" Cadence="90" />
        <IntervalsT Repeat="13" OnDuration="30" OffDuration="15" OnPower="1.10" OffPower="0.55" pace="0" Cadence="100" CadenceResting="85">
            <textevent timeoffset="0" message="Series 1 of 2 — 13 x 30/15. Settle on a power you can repeat to the end." />
        </IntervalsT>
        <SteadyState Duration="180" Power="0.55" pace="0" Cadence="90">
            <textevent timeoffset="0" message="Easy 3 min — spin, stay rolling." />
        </SteadyState>
        <IntervalsT Repeat="13" OnDuration="30" OffDuration="15" OnPower="1.10" OffPower="0.55" pace="0" Cadence="100" CadenceResting="85">
            <textevent timeoffset="0" message="Series 2 of 2 — 13 x 30/15. Same power as series 1." />
        </IntervalsT>
        <Cooldown Duration="480" PowerLow="0.55" PowerHigh="0.45" pace="0" Cadence="90" />
    </workout>
</workout_file>
